 "batch_notes": "Three debian-parsers crates, all with external oracles (dpkg Perl modules, python-debian, uscan's Perl, lintian's regex, apt) driven as persistent children. Recurring shape the rubric only half-captures: 'pin properties' that are generative but gated to one bug's domain (a fixed_instance() call plus `if !hits_X_bug(&g) return`), paired with general properties that gate *around* the same bugs (`if hits_X_bug return`); the general property is the one that plausibly found the bug and the gated one is a split-off, so I tagged the gated ones pin. Borderline cases (qualified_implication_agrees_with_dpkg, globs_match_newlines_like_python_debian) cover a whole sub-domain rather than one failure; tagged pin with medium confidence. Second recurring shape: 'follows the model' where the model is the generator's own record (the text was rendered from it), i.e. expected-by-construction; I classified those spec-postcondition and reserved model-oracle for in-test computations (glob matcher, find_files model, is_file_included rule). Corpus properties (real_*) sample an embedded corpus with tc.draw over an index rather than generating; several debian-watch pins pick from 3-7 literal cases and are barely generative. debian-control's 21 example pins are mostly panics on malformed fields and real-file layouts (UTC dates, dpkg-genbuildinfo Environment quoting, dak continuation lists) that no generator produced: the generators only emit well-formed values, so the malformed-input/robustness side is example-only. No known_bug_* naming convention here; docs cite bug ids and README says 'pinned' where a property is narrowed."
}